<section>
    <p>While there may be many reasons for poor quality of tech support (or customer service in a broader sense), but let's be honest,
        <em>the root cause</em> in most cases will be lack of investment. In the support team, in their tools, in the respective processes or a combination of those. The reason for that is straightforward: support is seen as an expenditure, something that doesn't generate any profit. For most businessmen, if something doesn't generate profit, you should drop it. If you can't do that for some reason, treat it as "necessary evil" and cut costs as much as possible.
    </p>
    <p>There's some logic there, indeed. Support teams normally do not "sell" anything, they do not code your program or build your hardware or craft your merchandise, they do not promote it. But it does not mean that they do not affect
        <em>the perception</em> that your clients have of the product. That perception is extremely challenging to measure, if not outright impossible. Support
        <em>is</em> part of your product whatever it may be, and often times can be even more important than the overall quality of that product.
    </p>
    <p>Cutting costs can lead to many things:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Lack of personnel</li>
        <li>Personnel don't know the product</li>
        <li>Personnel don't care about the product</li>
        <li>Lack of a centralized knowledgebase</li>
        <li>Highly repetitive tasks</li>
        <li>Tired personnel</li>
        <li>Unmotivated personnel</li>
        <li>Rude personnel</li>
    </ul>
    <img crossorigin="anonymous" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w50pc middle block gallery_zoom" src="/assets/images/supops/memes/overworked.avif" alt="Overworked" width="384" height="480">
    <p>Any of these result in poorly handled or even unresolved issues. Get enough of those for each user: they'll leave and never look back. Sometimes only 1 instance of poor support will be enough, especially if we're talking about physical products, doubly so, if they're expensive. If you are a monopolist or just one of the few options in your niche or a client just
        <em>has</em> to use your product for whatever reason, they can still stay. But they will <b>not</b> recommend you to their friends and colleagues, and once there is an alternative - will jump ship. Client churn, as you probably know, is something that all businesses prefer to avoid, but not getting new clients can be even worse for some businesses at certain stages.
    </p>
    <p>That's not the only result of poor support, though. It may not be obvious, but support is at the front line of data collection. They're the first that can see patterns of issues that you may not even know about, and places where you're actively losing money. Maybe not directly, but through "time wasted", for example, on repetitive tasks that the support team needs to do every day, instead of investigating more complex tickets. They can also gather information on how clients use the product and see potential shortcuts that can be implemented in the product if it's software, or just directly collecting feedback, that can be used to improve the product. You cut off the data flow, and it becomes more difficult to improve.</p>
    <p>But most importantly, good support can build <strong>trust</strong>. And trust results in
        <em>stickiness</em>, which is important for continuous services (whether subscriptions or something that requires periodic purchase). That is a client will less likely to leave,
        <em>even</em> if you <em>fail</em> at something. Because they know that you'll make things better.</p>
    <img crossorigin="anonymous" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w50pc middle block gallery_zoom" src="/assets/images/supops/memes/trust_me.avif" alt="Trust me" width="400" height="246">
    <p>Hopefully, you understand the importance of this, so let's discuss how we can approach this problem smartly.</p>
